A||N||8||MickeyAsh||Beretta brake upgrade - too much pedal play||||9||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||11-28-2000||01:04 AM||||Hey guys... well, I just got done with upgrading my rear calipers, pads and rotors to '90 Berettas... bled the system thoroughly, have clear fluid from the back two calipers... everything seems to be in good order, however, when I apply the brakes, the pedal travels almost all the way to the floor before the they actually catch... when they do finally catch, I come to a stop pretty quickly... I just dont feel comfortable with that much play in my pedal... any ideas? If I install a Blazer MC, does that increase or decrease the play in the pedal? Thanks in advance.<P>-Sheep||||reg||9
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||11-28-2000||02:06 AM||||With the new calipers you now need to move more fluid to make them move (bigger pistons) the new master cyl. displaces mor fluid for the same amount of travel. you should also get a proportioning valve to balance the system out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||wilburt||11-28-2000||03:50 PM||||It sounds like perhaps your front brakes aren't working at all, (or very little). I've owned Fieros with only the back brakes changed to Berettas, and haven't had the problem you mentioned. Make sure you bleed all four corners throughly, after making sure the front brakes work correctly. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||11-28-2000||05:40 PM||||It sound like your front brakes are working fine. I have just heard a lot about getting the new m. cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||11-28-2000||06:29 PM||||wilburt... how did the fiero drive with the GA/beretta brakes on the rear only?  just curious.<P>thanks<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000005||BBBAD GT||11-28-2000||08:50 PM||||Same thing happen to me with I did all 4 with GA brakes.  Put a full size blazer MC on and it helped alot, but now I think my rubber lines need to be replaced with SS units for even better feel.  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||afRaceR||11-28-2000||10:33 PM||||Did the fluid level ever get low in the resorvoir when you were bleeding the brakes? I would bleed the front as well just to make sure. If you have only done the back, I don't know that I would recommend getting a bigger M/C, I would just try a proportioning valve until you can do the front then add the M/C. You'll want the proportioning valve when you get the front done anyways. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||wilburt||11-28-2000||11:06 PM||||bHooper, The first Fiero that I put GA/Beretta rear brakes on, I really couldn't tell a difference between them and stock brakes. Also, I lost the parking brake, but it had an automatic. I still missed the parking brake though. <P>The real benefits of the GA/Beretta come from the front brakes, as they do most of the stopping. You can tell a huge improvement right away with the front brakes done.<P>With front and rear GA/Beretta brakes and proportioning valve, stock Fiero master cly., my brake pedal is maybe one or two inches lower than stock. Even with the machine work done on the rotors (front), its less expensive than the stock Fiero parts.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||11-29-2000||12:33 AM||||how much are steel braided lines? I want to replace all of my hoses etc with braided anyway, especially the fuel line(s)...<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
